      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 3,
          "section": "2.1 Mechanical properties",
          "quote": "The efficacy of SPS in grain refinement was demonstrated in two separate studies shown in Fig. 4. It can be seen that improvement in mechanical properties of the HEA produced by SPS is far higher than that produced via vacuum arc melting (VAM) in the production of Nb25Mo25Ta25W25 alloy [23,24].",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "d2020f6589e2a321:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sample_5",
      "original_name": "CoCrNiCuZn",
      "canonical_name": "CoCrNiCuZn",
      "alloy_system_id": "alloy_system_1",
      "material_state": "annealed at 900 °C",
      "composition": [],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 3,
          "section": "2.1 Mechanical properties",
          "quote": "Sun et al. [25] studied the mechanical properties of CoCrNiCuZn high entropy alloy consolidated with MA + SPS. ... However, after consolidation for 700 °C, 800 °C and 900 °C consecutively, the authors realized that the highest sintering temperature gave the best mechanical characteristics: Vickers hardness of 615 HV and compressive stress of 2121 MPa.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "d2020f6589e2a321:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sample_6",
      "original_name": "AlCoCrFeNi2.1",
      "canonical_name": "AlCoCrFeNi2.1",
      "alloy_system_id": "alloy_system_1",
      "material_state": "spark plasma sintered",
      "composition": [],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 3,
          "section": "2.1 Mechanical properties",
          "quote": "In a study by Pan et al. [26] to characterize AlCoCrFeNi2.1 produced via SPS of gas-atomized powders, it was discovered that as the sintering temperature increased, the porosity got depleted, while the mechanical properties improved subsequently.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "d2020f6589e2a321:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sample_7",
      "original_name": "TiZrNbTaMo",
      "canonical_name": "TiZrNbTaMo",
      "alloy_system_id": "alloy_system_1",
      "material_state": "spark plasma sintered",
      "composition": [],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 3,
          "section": "2.1 Mechanical properties",
          "quote": "In comparison of TiZrNbTaMo HEA prepared through arc melting technique with that synthesized via SPS, it was observed that the microstructure of arc melted HEA was BCC single phase while that produced with SPS consisted of BCC, FCC and ZrO2 phases.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "d2020f6589e2a321:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sample_8",
      "original_name": "FeCoNiAlSix",
      "canonical_name": "FeCoNiAlSix",
      "alloy_system_id": "alloy_system_1",
      "material_state": "spark plasma sintered",
      "composition": [],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 4,
          "section": "2.1 Mechanical properties",
          "quote": "Lee et al. [37] investigated the effects of Si addition to FeCoNiAl HEA produced via ball milling and SPS. The FeCoNiAlSix HEA ball milled powder showed only BCC phase. But when it was consolidated with SPS, there was evolution of dual phase-BCC + FCC. However, when Si was introduced into the HEA, the FCC changed into single phase BCC.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "d2020f6589e2a321:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
